## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted significant attention in recent years for their potential applications in gas storage, separations, and catalysis [1–5]. These materials have high surface areas, high pore volumes, and highly tunable structural properties. MOFs are synthesized via self-assembly with metal or metal oxides and organic bridging linkers to form crystalline networks. The wide variety of metal vertices and organic ligands allows for the design of materials with specific pore sizes and volumes, resulting in porous solids with prescribed properties [6–8]. MOFs containing unsaturated metal centers, or open-metal sites, have been used to develop materials with improved adsorption capacity for gas storage and separations. Recently, special focus has been placed on carbon emission reduction in coal or natural gas power plants via carbon dioxide separation from flue gas mixtures. Selective adsorption and good thermal stability make these microporous materials a potential candidate for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption separations [5,9–11], but the impact of water vapor on these features will also be important [12–15]. The presence of openmetal sites has a significant impact on adsorption behavior since it strongly favors the direct interaction between metal and adsorbate. Well-known MOFs, such as Mg-DOBDC and HKUST-1, possessing open-metal sites have shown among the highest adsorption performance for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ but are also known to adsorb up to $30\mathrm{mol/kg}$ of water under exposure to $90\%$ relative humidity (RH) [10,12].
The modular approach of MOFs allows their pore size and shape to be systematically tuned, not only by the choice of metal-containing secondary building units and/or bridging linkers, but also by different synthesis strategies. One strategy is the use of framework interpenetration or interweaving to achieve smaller pore sizes and stronger frameworks. The resulting MOFs possess high surface areas and frameworks with smaller pore diameters and higher pore volumes compared to the non-catenated forms, which enhance the adsorption potential of MOFs for certain adsorbates [16–20]. Chen et al. reported such an interwoven MOF $\mathrm{\langle[Cu_{3}(B T B)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}]}$ $(\mathrm{DMF})_{9}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}$ or MOF-14, where $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -benzene-1,3,5-triyl-tribenzoic acid ${\bf\Lambda}={\bf B}{\bf T}{\bf B}$ ) on a periodic minimal surface with extra-large pores [16], but no adsorption data were reported at that time.
As shown in Fig. 1, MOF-14 possesses a dicopper paddle wheel secondary building unit and BTB ligand. Each BTB is linked to three paddle wheel building units, and each paddle wheel building unit is connected to four BTB units. Such connections give rise to (3,4)- connected net with the $\mathsf{P t}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ topology (Fig. 2a). This unique structure is a pair of identical nets that are interwoven with each other (Fig. 2b). The rings of one net are penetrated by links of the other, so that they are truly catenated. Each net is involved in numerous $\pi{-}\pi$ and $\operatorname{C-H}\cdot\cdot\pi$ interactions with those of the adjacent interpenetrated net. The pore limiting diameter and largest cavity diameter were estimated as 5.2 and $16.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively, using the geometric approach algorithm [21]. The removal of the bound water molecules gives rise to open-metal sites, which are important in increasing binding strength between the framework and guest molecules. However, these preferred interactions are magnified in the case of water adsorption, and this competition for adsorption sites can lower the adsorption selectivity for the molecules of interest. There is also a concern for the MOF structural integrity, since water may degrade the framework [12–15].
In this study, we have investigated the adsorption properties of MOF-14 and the effect of water exposure on the structure. Purecomponent isotherms were measured for carbon dioxide, methane, nitrogen, and water vapor. These isotherms were then used in the Ideal Adsorption Solution Theory (IAST) to calculate mixture adsorption properties for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ . To the best of our knowledge, an examination of the adsorption properties of this MOF has not been presented yet. MOF-14 is shown to exhibit high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ selectivity over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and is shown to adsorb just one-tenth of the amount of water compared to $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC and Mg-MOF-74 [12].
## 2. Materials synthesis and characterization
All chemicals were used as received from commercial suppliers without further purification. The reported synthesis procedure was slightly modified [16]. A mixture of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\cdot30.2\mathrm{mg}$ $0.125\mathrm{{mmol}}$ ), BTB $(54.8~\mathrm{mg},~0.125~\mathrm{mmol})$ ), and pyrazine ( $10\mathrm{mg},$ $0.125\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) was dissolved in dimethylformamide (DMF) $(5~\mathrm{ml})$ with the addition of two drops of $1\ N\ N{\mathsf{a O H}}$ at room temperature.
The slurry was transferred to stainless steel reactors and hydrothermally treated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $96\mathrm{h}$ . Green cubic crystals were obtained with the molecular formula $\{[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T B})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}]$ $(\mathrm{DMF})_{9}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}\}$ , confirmed by elemental and thermogravimetric analyses and compared with the previously reported crystal structure [16]. The synthesized compound was solvent exchanged with chloroform for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ to remove DMF.
Nitrogen adsorption isotherms were measured at 77 K $(-196^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ with a Quadrasorb SI analyzer from Quantachrome Instruments. Solvent exchanged samples were degassed via evacuation at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under $10^{-5}$ Torr dynamic vacuum overnight to remove residue from the pore structure. The Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area was determined in the range $0.007<P/P_{\mathrm{o}}<0.05$ . ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption experiments were carried out at 278 K, 298 K, and $318\mathrm{K}$ using the Intelligent Gravimetric Analyzer (IGA-001 series, Hiden Analytical Ltd.). The samples were activated overnight at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under vacuum. Each adsorption/desorption step was allowed to reach equilibrium over a period of $20{-}40\mathrm{min}$ , until no further weight gain was detected.
Water adsorption measurements were carried out at $298\mathrm{K}$ $(25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ using an Intelligent Gravimetric Analyzer (IGA-003 series, Hiden Analytical Ltd). The sample was activated prior to the run overnight ( $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under vacuum). Dry air was used as the carrier gas, with a portion of the carrier gas being bubbled through a vessel of deionized water. The relative humidity (RH) was controlled by varying the ratio of saturated air and dry air via two mass flow controllers, where the total gas flow rate was $200\mathrm{cc/min}$ for the duration of the experiment. The maximum humidity level examined was $90\%$ RH due to condensation occurring inside the apparatus at higher humidities. Each adsorption/desorption step was allowed to approach equilibrium over a period of $2{-}24\mathrm{h}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structural characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis of MOF-14 is shown in Fig. 3. The samples were analyzed step-by-step, starting from the as-synthesized MOF, and then followed by samples that were chloroform exchanged, activated, water exposed, regenerated again after water exposure, and finally as a resolvated compound. The activated sample was obtained by drying the chloroform-exchanged sample at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under vacuum for overnight, while the regenerated sample was obtained by subjecting the water vapor exposed sample to the same heat treatment conditions as activated sample. The resolvated compound was obtained by immersing the regenerated sample in DMF for two days. The PXRD pattern is almost identical with the simulated pattern for MOF-14, and the collected cell parameters also correspond to the structure previously reported by Chen et al. [16]. The PXRD pattern of the guest-free phase and chloroform-exchanged phase presents the same structure as the as-synthesized compound. Furthermore, the PXRD of the water-exposed sample was not significantly different from the activated sample, but some minimal changes in peak intensity were observed after repeated thermal activation performed at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$
Thermogravimetric analysis data show that the structure is thermally stable to almost $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The material releases its guest molecules in the temperature range $25\mathrm{-}250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to form the guestfree phase (see Fig. S1). Elemental analysis was performed to examine the purity of the MOF by analyzing the concentration of carbon, hydrogen, and nitrogen elements and comparing it to a theoretical MOF-14 sample. The calculated values of carbon, hydrogen, and nitrogen are very close to the synthesized material (Table S1), which confirms the good quality the sample. Nitrogen adsorption measurements performed at $77\mathrm{K}\left(-196^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ on the activated sample exhibit Type I isotherm behavior (Fig. S2), which is typical for microporous materials with high surface area. The permanent porosity of MOF-14 was confirmed by the calculated BET surface area and total pore volume of $1398\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.57\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively.
## 3.2. Pure-component adsorption isotherms at $298K$
Pure-component adsorption isotherms of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ on MOF-14 were measured at $298\mathrm{K}$ up to 20 bar. Fig. 4 shows the adsorption isotherms with loadings following the order $\ C0_{2}>$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}>\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . Carbon dioxide has the highest quadrupole moment of the aforementioned gases $(4.30\times10^{-26}\mathrm{esucm}^{2})$ , followed by nitrogen $(1.52\times10^{-26}\mathrm{esucm^{2}};$ , and finally nonpolar methane [22]. However, the results do not exactly reflect such a trend. Carbon dioxide adsorbs at significantly higher loadings, but the loadings for the other two gases follow an opposite trend. Electrostatic interactions play an important role in such gas adsorption behavior. Methane has a higher polarizability in comparison with nitrogen, which causes higher surface interactions affected by polarity. These trends have also been observed for $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC, which possesses open copper sites similar to MOF-14 [23,24]. The methane adsorption isotherm is slightly lower than the simulated adsorption data reported by Gallo et al. [19]. This overestimation is likely associated with crystal defects or impurities present in the experimental samples, which are not accounted for in the model. MOF-14 adsorbs $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ up to ${\sim}2.5\mathrm{mol/kg}$ at $0.1\mathrm{MPa}$ and $298\mathrm{K}$ , which is lower than the loading observed for Cu-BTC $(\sim5\mathrm{mol/kg})$ [24] under the same conditions due to the more easily accessible pore space in Cu-BTC, which is a non-catenated MOF. However, the adsorption capacities are higher than those of other MOFs such as IRMOF-1 $\left(\sim1.1\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ [25], IRMOF-3 $(\sim1.3\mathrm{mol/kg})$ [25], open-metal site MOF Cu-BTB $(2\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{kg})$ [26], and DMOF $({\sim}2.2\ \mathrm{mol/kg})$ [24] under the same conditions. At higher pressures, the desolvated MOF adsorbs ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ up to ${\sim}11\ \mathrm{mol/kg}$ at $2\mathsf{M P a}$ , due to its large pore volume and surface area.
## 3.3. Analysis of heat of adsorption
The isosteric heat of adsorption $\left(q_{S T}\right)$ at zero loading reflects the strength of interaction between the framework and adsorbate molecule. The isosteric heat for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ was calculated by applying the Clausius–Clapeyron equation (Eq. (1)) to isotherms taken at 278, 298, and 318 K (Fig. S3).
$$
\frac{q_{S T}}{R T^{2}}=-\bigg(\frac{\partial\ln P}{\partial T}\bigg)_{n}
$$
The isosteric heat as a function of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ loading is shown in Fig. 5. The heat of adsorption approaches 26 kJ/mol near zero loading and decreases with increasing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ loading. This indicates that the electrostatic interactions between the quadrupole moment of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and the exposed copper sites at low gas concentrations are dominant, as seen for other open-metal site MOFs [9,17,18,23,27,28], and the values are similar to those obtained for Cu-BTC [24].
## 3.4. Water vapor adsorption
Water adsorption measured on MOF-14 at $298\mathrm{K}$ is shown in Fig. 6. The amount of water vapor adsorbed increases gradually with a rise in the relative humidity. The isotherm is irreversible and exhibits a large hysteresis loop. There is some amount of water retained in the pores of MOF-14 after desorption, even after the sample is exposed to dry air at the final stage of the experiment. The water vapor adsorption capacity is $3\mathrm{mol/kg}\left(5.3\mathrm{wt.\%}\right)$ at $90\%$ relative humidity. This is in sharp contrast to Cu-BTC and MgMOF-74, which adsorb 30 and $35\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{kg}$ of water, respectively, under the same conditions [12]. The amount of water irreversibly bound in MOF-14 is approximately $1.45\mathrm{mol/kg}$ . This corresponds to 0.84 water molecules per formula unit, which is equivalent to 0.28 water molecules per copper center. In comparison, water loadings in Cu-BTC under the same experimental conditions result in 0.69 water molecules per copper center [12].
In order to better understand the reason for the lower water vapor uptake of MOF-14, the water vapor exposed sample was characterized by nitrogen adsorption measurements performed at $77\mathrm{K}$ and by PXRD to examine its possible structural degradation. The PXRD patterns did not show major changes after water exposure and regeneration. However, the patterns did indicate a decrease in its peak intensities after water exposure and after regeneration compared to its pristine activated form. This suggests that some of the pores may have collapsed upon water exposure. The partial pore collapse might have been from the weakening of some of the copper–oxygen bonds of the BTB linker upon interaction with water vapor. However, it is important to note that PXRD analysis may not show small transformations in material crystallinity. Moreover, nitrogen adsorption analysis at $77\mathrm{K}$ reveals a significant surface area loss. The BET surface area was drastically reduced from 1398 to $643~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ after water exposure. This suggests that after water exposure in air and thermal regeneration, it is difficult to completely remove the adsorbed water molecules, which are strongly bound to copper atoms, without damaging the structure. Similar behavior has been observed for Cu-BTC [18], which showed $26\%$ surface area loss compared to $54\%$ reduction in surface area for MOF-14.
## 3.5. Binary mixture adsorption calculations
Adsorption equilibrium data and adsorption selectivities for equimolar mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ were calculated using the Ideal Adsorbed Solution Theory (IAST). IAST is a well-known method for calculating mixture adsorption isotherms using purecomponent isotherm data. It has been shown to accurately predict gas mixture adsorption in many zeolites and MOFs [29]. In calculation of adsorption selectivities for the binary mixtures through the IAST method, the Toth equation was used as the best fit for the pure-component experimental data. Model parameters are given in Table S2. The adsorption separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ mixtures is quantified by calculating the adsorption selectivity $S_{i j}=\left(x_{i}/\right.$ $x_{j})(y_{j}/y_{i}).$ , where $x_{i}$ and $y_{i}$ are the mole fractions of component i in the adsorbed and bulk gas phases, respectively.
Adsorption isotherms predicted by IAST for equimolar mixtures of ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ in MOF-14 as a function of total bulk pressure are shown in Figs. 7 and 8, respectively. The existence of catenation in this interwoven copper MOF affects gas adsorption phenomena via enhancement of van der Waals interactions with adsorbent molecules. The smaller pore diameter causes an increase in adsorption energies between the adsorbate (gas) and adsorbent wall, which leads to a porous material with more selective adsorption properties (Figs. 7 and 8). ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is preferentially adsorbed over $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ over the entire pressure range considered. ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption is essentially unaffected by the presence of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ in the mixture compared to the pure-component isotherm as shown in Fig. 7, but the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ loadings are significantly lower in the mixture. A slight decrease in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ loadings is observed at higher pressures in the case of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixtures (Fig. 8). These differences in mixture behavior compared to pure-component can be attributed to the types of interactions occurring within the system. ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ will experience the highest electrostatic interactions with the open-metal sites due to the quadrupole moment, but van der Waals interactions due to the catenated framework will also be significant contributors [22,24]. Neither $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ nor $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ will interact as strongly with the open-metal sites compared to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Thus, at low pressure, where sorbate–sorbent interactions dominate, $\Nu_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ will not compete effectively with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ for adsorption sites. However, since the pore limiting diameter of catenated MOF-14 is only $5.1\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ the enhanced adsorption potential for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , or high van der Waals interactions, is enough to increase the adsorption competition such that selectivities for the equimolar mixtures remain modest, and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ loadings are slightly depressed at high pressures compared to the purecomponent isotherm [24,26].
Adsorption selectivities for both mixtures are shown in Fig. 9 as a function of total pressure. The selectivity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over ${\sf N}_{2}$ is 32 in the low-pressure region and then decreases to 19 with increasing total pressure. This trend occurs due to favorable interactions between ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules and the open-metal sites at lower pressures. As loadings increase, the sites become occupied and van der Waals interactions will dominate the adsorption behavior, resulting in greater competition between the mixture species [24]. The selectivity for ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ separation at $0.1\mathrm{MPa}$ is 22 and is slightly higher than values reported for porous materials including zeolite 4A (18.8) [30], activated carbon Norit R1 (15.3) [31], Cu-BTC (20) [24], and MOF-508b (range 3–6) [32] at similar conditions. The selectivity for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at 0.1 MPa is approximately 4. The observed result is in the same range as other MOFs such as IRMOF-1 (range 2–3) [33], Cu-BTC (6–9) [33], MOF-508b (3–6) [32]. It appears that although ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ interacts more strongly with open-metal sites compared to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , the small pore size from the catenated framework and available pore volume result in an adsorption potential that is overall favorable for the adsorption of both gases, which depresses the selectivity [26]. It would be expected that for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ mixtures with higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ compositions, selectivities will be higher than those exhibited here [26].
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, this study has demonstrated the significance of catenated metal–organic frameworks for potential applications in gas separation. We have reported an adsorption study of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and equimolar mixtures of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ on MOF-14. We obtained high carbon dioxide loadings on the material $(\sim2.5\mathrm{mol/kg}$ at $0.1\mathrm{MPa}$ bar and 298 K) due to its small pore size, high pore volume, and high BET specific surface area. Mixture adsorption results exhibited higher adsorption selectivities for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ in comparison with the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas pair. Moreover, we examined the influence of water exposure on MOF-14. PXRD patterns appeared to be unchanged for the water-exposed samples, but the BET surface area was significantly reduced $54\%$ loss). Notably, MOF-14 adsorbs less than one-tenth of the amount of water adsorbed by other well-known MOFs with open-metal sites such as Cu-BTC and Mg-MOF-74. Overall, the small pores and open-metal sites of MOF-14 lead to high selectivity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , but the water adsorption is relatively small. These features make MOF-14 a promising porous material for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption.
